47, "incumbency": -3.0, "money": 0.53}, "rating_margin": 1.82, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.7, "baseline_margin": 2.91, "analyst_adjustment": -0.31, "key_factors": ["October polls from Marist, Siena, SSRS and YouGov all show Brown ahead by three to six points", "Strong anti-Trump midterm environment in a state Republicans have dominated recently, with GOP turnout worries", "Husted's incumbency and Ohio's Republican lean keep the race within tossup range for most raters", "Brown's large fundraising edge is partly offset by heavy outside spending against him"], "rationale": "High-quality October polls consistently put Brown up three to six points, prediction markets price him near two-to-one, and several raters call the race a Democratic lean, though Husted's incumbency and Ohio's Republican tilt cap the edge.", "watch": "A Republican turnout surge or a late shift on affordability and data-center issues could erase Brown's narrow lead.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Jon Husted (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Ohio's special Senate race pits appointed Republican incumbent Jon Husted against former three-term senator Sherrod Brown, and it has become one of the cycle's most contested contests. Polling through mid-October consistently shows Brown ahead by roughly three to six points, with even Republican-leaning pollsters finding the race tied or close. Eleven race raters split between tossup and lean Democratic, and prediction markets give Brown about a two-in-three chance.\n\nBrown has raised far more money than Husted, though outside groups have spent heavily against both candidates. Husted benefits from incumbency and a state that has trended Republican in recent years, and Republicans are working to improve turnout. The race has centered on affordability, data centers and Trump-era politics. Brown enters the final weeks with a modest edge, while meaningful uncertainty remains about turnout and any late break toward the incumbent.", "history": [